      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring 1 litre of water to the boil in a large saucepan. Add the lemongrass, galangal, kaffir lime leaves, shallots, and chilli."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Simmer for 10 minutes to build the broth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the prawns. Cook for 2 to 3 minutes until pink and curled."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from the heat. Season with {{fish-sauce}} and lime juice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste and adjust the balance. Tom yum should be hot, sour, salty, and lightly fragrant. Add more lime for sour, more fish sauce for salt."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Ladle into bowls and top with spring onions and coriander. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Soup tastes flat",
              "cause": "Not enough lime juice or fish sauce",
              "fix": "Add lime juice one tablespoon at a time until the sourness lifts the broth."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Prawns are rubbery",
              "cause": "Overcooked",
              "fix": "Next time, add prawns off the heat and let the residual heat cook them gently over 2 minutes."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Broth is too spicy",
              "cause": "Too many chillies or seeds left in",
              "fix": "Add a squeeze of lime and a pinch of caster sugar to balance. Remove chilli pieces before serving."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Tom yum is one of Thailand's most famous exports. It appears on menus from street corners in Chiang Mai to fine dining rooms in Bangkok. The soup has no single recipe. Each cook adjusts the balance of hot, sour, and salty to their own taste. Lemongrass, galangal, and kaffir lime leaves are always present. The prawns are a classic choice but the broth works equally well with chicken or fish. This is a dish built on fragrance more than technique."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
